  "dom_id": "step-q72829",
  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040762/assets/6e2223a7dadd9aeddad0b029bf2ba8fc18bafc74dcaca88346ee2b702029b597.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the curve $y=\\operatorname{tan}\u2061x$ and the line $y=-\\sqrt{3}.$  Find the $x$-coordinate of the point $B.$",
  "worked_solution": "The $y$-values of both functions are the same at the intersection point. Equating the two equations gives:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}\u2061x=-\\sqrt{3}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arctan}\u2061(-\\sqrt{3})=-\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis value lies inside $-\\pi \\le x<\\pi $, hence, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$ This is the $x$ coordinate of $A.$\n\nTo get another solution, we add $\\pi $ to the principal value. Thus,\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=\\pi +x=\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{3}=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nSo, the $x$-coordinate of $B$ is  $\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.$",
